board of yard and lot performance, inventory, feeding, weight, cost, and energy values and let users select the measure used to rank best- and worst-performing lots.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-15\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-9\",\n    \"ASM-14\",\n    \"ASM-22\",\n    \"ASM-36\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 0.6666666666666666\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The operational report service returns records for multiple pens, lots, rations, feed plans, inventory items, and feed costs\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-16.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen shows which pen consumed the most feed, which lot has the best FCR and highest ADG, which ration is used most, planned-versus-actual feed and over- or under-feeding pens, remaining inventory, and feed cost per unit of weight gain\",\n     \"when\": \"A user opens the reports screen and applies its available filters\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A filtered operational report is displayed\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-16.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The downloaded file contains the filtered report rows shown on the screen\",\n     \"when\": \"The user exports the report as CSV\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide filterable operational reports for inventory, feeding, lot performance, and profitability that answer the requested pen, lot, ration, plan-variance, inventory, and cost-of-gain questions and export the filtered results as CSV.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-16\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-9\",\n    \"ASM-17\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 0.3333333333333333\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The service response contains only records for the user's authorized yards and profitability fields allowed by the user's returned permissions\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-17.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen shows only the returned authorized-yard records and permitted profitability fields\",\n     \"when\": \"The user opens a dashboard or report\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A user exports a filtered dashboard or report view\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-17.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The downloaded file contains only the authorized-yard records and profitability fields shown to that user\",\n     \"when\": \"The export is generated\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A dashboard or report displays records or profitability fields and an updated permissions response removes access to some of that data\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-17.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen no longer displays the records or fields no longer permitted, and a subsequent downloaded file contains only currently permitted records and fields\",\n     \"when\": \"The web app processes the updated permissions\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display dashboards, reports, and exports using only records and profitability fields returned for the signed-in user's authorized yards and permissions, and remove previously displayed data from view and subsequent exports when returned permissions change.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-17\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-9\",\n    \"I-10\",\n    \"Q-1\",\n    \"ASM-14\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The report service returns lot records with weight and feeding data for a selected date range\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-18.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The report request contains the selected filters and the screen displays matching lots with ADG, FCR, and cost-of-gain values calculated from records in the selected date range\",\n     \"when\": \"The user selects date-range, breed, sex, or pen filters\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"Records without sex data are included in the report result set\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-18.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the sex filter as unavailable for records without sex data\",\n     \"when\": \"The user views the sex filter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The user has applied report filters and has export access\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-18.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The downloaded file contains the filtered report rows shown on the screen and omits any yard or profitability fields not permitted by returned permissions\",\n     \"when\": \"The user exports the report as CSV or PDF\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide a feed-to-gain report comparing lot ADG, FCR, and cost of gain, with date-range, breed, sex, and pen filters, and CSV or PDF exports of filtered results subject to the user's returned permissions.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-18\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-10\",\n    \"ASM-21\",\n    \"ASM-35\",\n    \"Q-1\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The service returns profitability rows for yards authorized to the user\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-19.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays only the returned authorized-yard rows and their available profitability values\",\n     \"when\": \"The user opens and filters the lot profitability view\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The user has applied filters to the authorized lot profitability list\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-19.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The downloaded file contains the filtered authorized rows displayed by the screen\",\n     \"when\": \"The user selects CSV export\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The service indicates that the user cannot view profitability values\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-19.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen hides profitability values\",\n     \"when\": \"The user opens the lot profitability view\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide a filterable on-screen lot profitability list for authorized yards and display a CSV file containing the filtered authorized results when the user exports the list.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-19\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-1\",\n    \"I-9\",\n    \"Q-1\",\n    \"ASM-17\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 0.3333333333333333\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"Sample data is available and has not been loaded\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-20.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned sample records with a visible sample-data designation distinguishing them from operational records\",\n     \"when\": \"An authorized user loads the sample data\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"Sample data is already loaded or a duplicate load is refused\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-20.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned duplicate-load result and the returned record list contains no duplicate sample records\",\n     \"when\": \"A user attempts to load sample data\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A user starts a sample-data reset or replacement but has not confirmed\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-20.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen shows the existing sample records unchanged and the reset or replacement request is not sent\",\n     \"when\": \"The confirmation dialog is displayed and the user cancels\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A user confirms a sample-data reset or replacement\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-20.4\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned operation status and resulting sample-data list or record count when supplied\",\n     \"when\": \"The service returns the operation result\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A user lacks sample-data management permission and opens the sample-data controls\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-20.5\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays sample records and does not show enabled load, reset, or replacement actions\",\n     \"when\": \"The screen renders using the user's returned permissions\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The service refuses a sample-data load, reset, or replacement because the user lacks permission\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-20.6\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned error and the existing sample-data list remains displayed\",\n     \"when\": \"The user submits the operation\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The service response contains both sample and operational records for dashboards, reports, inventory, or a yard summary\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-20.7\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays operational values based only on operational records and labels any sample records shown as sample data\",\n     \"when\": \"A user opens one of those operational views\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A user exports an operational dashboard, report, inventory, or yard-summary view when the service response contains sample and operational records\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-20.8\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The downloaded file contains operational records only and excludes sample records\",\n     \"when\": \"The user generates the export\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall let an authorized user load visibly distinct sample data, prevent duplicate sample loading while displaying the returned result, require confirmation before sample-data reset or replacement, and distinguish sample records from operational records while excluding sample records from operational dashboard, report, inventory-total, yard-summary, and export results.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-20\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-11\",\n    \"I-12\",\n    \"ASM-15\",\n    \"ASM-37\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The summary service returns active-lot aggregate values\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-21.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays total head, average ADG, average cost of gain, and total feed cost for the returned active lots\",\n     \"when\": \"A user with yard-summary access opens the yard summary\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The summary service returns no data for one or more summary metrics\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-21.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays returned available values and an explicit unavailable value for each missing field\",\n     \"when\": \"A user opens the yard summary\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display a yard-level summary of active-lot total head, average ADG, average cost of gain, and total feed cost using returned aggregate values, and show unavailable values when required source data is missing.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-21\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-11\",\n    \"ASM-16\",\n    \"ASM-38\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The delivery overview or presentation page is available\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-22.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays sections or links for the proposed solution, architecture diagrams, app demo entry point, prompts used, testing and validation, challenges, production readiness, and go-to-market analysis\",\n     \"when\": \"A user opens the page\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide a delivery overview or presentation page containing the proposed solution, architecture diagrams, app demo entry point, prompts used, testing and validation overview, challenges, production-readiness assessment, and go-to-market analysis.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-22\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-12\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  }\n ]\n}\n</artifact>\n\n<artifact id=\"decisions\" kind=\"decisions\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"id\": \"Q-9\",\n  \"question\": \"Should all 22 requirements be approved as one run, or should some requirements be cut?\",\n  \"answer\": \"Approve all 22 requirements as one run.\",\n  \"settles\": [\n   \"L9 size: This spec has 22 requirements, about 3 runs' worth of work for a feature; approve it as one run or reject with which part to cut.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"Q-10\",\n  \"question\": \"How should the app handle a feeding save that times out when it cannot reliably tell whether the event was saved?\",\n  \"answer\": \"Remove the uncertain-save reconciliation requirement; show a timed-out save as unknown and leave retry resolution to the existing service/user workflow.\",\n  \"settles\": [\n   \"AC-11.8–11.9 treats matching event fields in a refreshed activity list as proof of whether a timed-out save succeeded, so an older identical event can be mistaken for the new save and a delayed or omitted event can lead to a duplicate feeding and inventory decrement.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"Q-11\",\n  \"question\": \"Should dashboard and yard-summary aggregates be guaranteed to exclude sample records, even when the service returns aggregates mixed with operational data?\",\n  \"answer\": \"Display the returned aggregate values as provided, without a sample-free guarantee.\",\n  \"settles\": [\n   \"AC-20.7 requires sample-free dashboard and yard-summary totals even when the response mixes sample and operational records, while REQ-15 and REQ-21 require displaying returned summary and aggregate values, leaving no consistent client behavior if those returned aggregates include sample data.\"\n  ]\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\n<untrusted_document id=\"request\" source=\"cli\">\nHackathon Problem Statement: Feedlot Management Solution\n\nA feedlot is a specialized livestock operation focused on transforming young cattle (300–500 lbs)\ninto market-ready livestock (1,200–1,400 lbs) over an 8- to 12-month cycle. The core business\nobjective is to maximize the \"feed-to-gain\" ratio, as the operation's profitability is directly tied\nto how efficiently animals convert nutritional rations into body weight.\n\nManagement is split between two primary tiers: Lots and Pens. A \"Lot\" reflects the business\nunit (a specific group of cattle purchased and analyzed together for performance), while a \"Pen\"\nserves as the physical unit (where daily feeding and housing activities occur). Effective software\nmust bridge these tiers, allowing for granular daily execution at the pen level while providing\nhigh-level performance metrics at the lot level.\n\nKey Requirements\n● Integrated Data Management\n○ Procurement Ingestion: Seamlessly import animal data (weight, breed, age, and\nsource) from the primary procurement system.\n○ Lifecycle Forecasting: Automatically calculate projected market dates and target\nweights based on incoming data.\n● Feed & Ration Management\n○ Inventory Control & Projections: Monitor real-time ingredient levels and\ngenerate automated inventory forecasts across multiple time horizons—daily,\nweekly, monthly, quarterly, bi-annual, and annual.\n○ Wastage Calibration: Integrate a configurable wastage ratio into all consumption\nestimates and inventory projections to account for feeding-loss and ensure\nprocurement accuracy.\n○ Ration & Cost Optimization: Manage complex nutritional recipes and perform\ncost-benefit analysis on different feed mixes to identify the most economical\npaths to weight gain.\n○ Dynamic Bunk Management: Track daily bunk rates and consumption patterns at\nthe pen level, allowing for real-time adjustments to feeding plans based on\nactual intake versus projections.\n● Execution & Activity Tracking\n○ Pen-Level Planning: Generate daily feeding schedules and plans for specific\nphysical pens.\n○ Activity Logging: Capture actual feeding data to identify variances between the\nplan and execution.\n● Performance & Conversion Analytics\n\n○ Feed-to-Gain Ratio: Calculate the primary efficiency KPI (Total Feed Consumed vs.\nTotal Weight Gained).\n○ Lot-Level Profitability: Provide deep-dive analysis on specific groups to determine\nthe financial ROI of each lot.\n○ Predictive Insights: Suggest \"next steps\" for feeding adjustments or sale timing\nbased on current weight-gain trends.\n\nAdditional Information & Requirements:\n\nCreate sample/mock data to support the system. Assume the following information is based on\nmock data fetched from other systems.\n\n● Automated Data Ingestion: Synchronize PO and Receiving data (Vendor, Costs, Head\nCount, Weights) to eliminate manual entry and establish the financial baseline for each\nlot.\n● Dynamic Lot Mapping: Reflect complex upstream relationships where a single PO Line\nmay split into multiple Lots, ensuring 1:1 or 1:N traceability.\n● Entity Mapping (PO → Lot → Pen): Provide the foundation to link procurement data to\nphysical locations. The system will reflect Pen ID, capacity, and current animal counts as\nfetched from the source.\n● Centralized Operational Entities: Establish the Lot and Pen as the primary units for all\ndownstream feeding and performance analytics.\n● Lightweight Implementation: Focus on backend data connectivity rather than refined\nUI/UX, as these modules serve specifically to support the feed-to-gain engine.\n\nThe Feed-to-Gain Engine (The Core Challenge)\n\nInventory, Ration Module & Feed Plan per Pen\n\n● Allow users to maintain a feed Inventory with feed types, available quantities, and unit\ncosts ($/lb or $/ton).\n● Each inventory item may include:feed item name, feed category, quantity available, unit\nof measure, cost per unit, dry matter percentage, energy value, and storage location. It\nshould also track stock movement by maintaining the opening stock, stock received,\nstock consumed, and closing stock for each feed item.\n\n● Allow users to create a Ration (a feed recipe) by combining multiple feed ingredients\nwith specified proportions. Each ration has a calculated cost per pound based on its\ningredient mix.\n● A ration may include the Ration name, Ration type, Feed ingredients used, Quantity or\npercentage of each ingredient, Dry matter percentage, Energy value, Cost per unit, and\nIntended animal type or weight range.\n● A feed plan per pen defines what ration needs to be fed to a specific pen, how much\nfeed should be provided, and on which date it should be delivered. Since pens may\ncontain animals from one or more lots, the feed plan helps operators manage feeding\nexecution in a structured and organized way. The feed plan per pen may include the\nFeed plan date, Pen, Assigned lot, Number of animals in the pen, Selected ration,\nPlanned feed quantity, Planned feeding time, Feed quantity per animal, Total feed cost,\nExpected energy intake, and Feed plan status, such as Planned, In progress, Completed,\nor Skipped. The system should allow users to generate a feed plan based on the daily\nbunk rate, selected ration, and number of animals in the pen.\n\nFeeding Activity\n\n● Allow users to log a Feeding Activity against a specific Pen, capturing:\n○ The feeding activity should capture the Feeding date, Pen, Lot, Ration, Planned\nfeed quantity, Actual feed quantity delivered, Difference between planned and\nactual feed, Feed cost, Energy value, Inventory consumed, and Feeding status.\n● On save, the system should:\n○ Decrement the feed inventory based on the ration's ingredient mix\n○ Calculate and store the total feed cost for that feeding event\n○ Roll up cumulative feed consumption and feed cost at the lot level\n● Suggested calculation: Feed Variance = Actual Feed Quantity - Planned Feed Quantity\n\nWeight Measurement Activity\n\n● Allow users to log a Weight Measurement Activity against a Lot, capturing:\n○ Lot\n○ Measurement date\n○ Number of animals weighed\n○ Average animal weight\n○ Total lot weight\n○ Weight gain since last measurement\n○ Notes\n● Maintain a time-series of weight readings per lot to enable trend analysis.\n\nFeed-to-Gain Calculation Engine\n\nThis is the most critical part. Build the calculation logic that derives the following metrics from\nfeeding and weight data:\n\nFeed-to-Gain Calculation Metrics\n\nMetric Formula Why It Matters\n\nDays on Feed (DOF) Current Date − Receiving Date Tracks how long the lot has\nbeen on feed\n\nTotal Weight Gain Current Avg Weight − Entry Avg Weight Total growth achieved so far\n\nAverage Daily Gain (ADG) Total Weight Gain ÷ DOF Primary performance\nbenchmark\n\nTotal Feed Consumed Sum of all feeding events Total feed input\n\nFeed Conversion Ratio\n(FCR)\nTotal Feed Consumed (lbs) ÷ Total\nWeight Gain (lbs)\nFeed efficiency metric (lower\nis better)\n\nCost of Gain ($/lb) Total Feed Cost ÷ Total Weight Gain Core profitability indicator\n\nProjected Ship Date DOF + ((Target Weight − Current Weight)\n÷ ADG)\nForecasted shipping readiness\n\nThese metrics should update automatically whenever a new feeding or weight event is\nrecorded.\n\nDashboard & Reports (Visualization Layer)\n\nSurface the engine's output in a way that helps a feedlot operator make decisions.\n\nThe dashboard should include:\n\n● Active lots\n● Active pens\n● Animals per lot and pen\n● Feed inventory status\n● Ration usage\n● Daily bunk rate per pen\n● Planned feed vs actual feed\n● Total feed consumed\n● Total feed cost\n● Starting weight vs current weight\n● Total weight gain\n● Average Daily Gain\n● Feed-to-gain ratio\n● Feed cost per unit of gain\n● Energy consumed\n● Best and worst performing lots\n\nThe reports should help answer questions such as:\n\n● Which pen consumed the most feed?\n● Which lot has the best feed-to-gain ratio?\n● Which lot has the highest Average Daily Gain?\n● Which ration is being used the most?\n● Is actual feeding aligned with the feed plan?\n● Which pens are being overfed or underfed?\n● How much feed inventory is remaining?\n● What is the feed cost per unit of weight gain?\n\nFeed-to-Gain Report\n\nA dedicated report screen that allows users to:\n\n● Compare ADG, FCR, and Cost of Gain across multiple lots\n● Filter by date range, breed, sex, or pen\n\n● Export the report as a CSV or PDF\n\nYard-Level Summary (Stretch Goal)\n\nA high-level dashboard showing aggregate metrics across all active lots — total head, average\nADG, average Cost of Gain, total feed cost — to give the yard manager a single-glance overview.\n\nEvaluation Criteria\n\nSubmissions will be evaluated on:\n\n1. Correctness of the Feed-to-Gain Calculations — Are the formulas implemented\naccurately? Do metrics update correctly as new data is entered?\n2. Quality of the Dashboard & Visualizations — How effectively does the UI communicate\nperformance to a non-technical operator?\n3. Data Integrity — Are inventory decrements, feed cost calculations, and weight tracking\nconsistent and reliable?\n4. User Experience — Is the flow from PO → Lot → Daily Activities → Dashboard intuitive\nand frictionless?\n5. Code Quality & Architecture — Is the solution modular and extensible for future\nactivities (treatments, vaccinations, shipping, etc.)?\n\nWhat to present and deliver:\n● Proposed Solution (Approach) and Architectural Diagrams\n● Demo of the App\n● Overview Vibe Coding Super Prompt & Other prompts\n● How was it tested and validated?\n● What challenges did you face?\n● How close is this to production readiness?\n● Go to Market Strategy and Analysis (Extra Advantage) If you build a product based\nsolution/offering & with a go-to-market insight.\n</untrusted_document>\n\nEach decision answers a question about a problem in this spec. Change the spec so it does exactly what each answer says: add or change requirements and acceptance criteria, cite the answer id (Q-n) in \"sources\", and remove what an answer leaves out. Keep requirement IDs stable where the meaning doesn't change. Change nothing the decisions don't need. Never move requested behaviour out of scope or delete the requirements that cover an intent span.\nAnswer with the changes only, not the whole spec: \"requirements\" holds each requirement you change or add, whole, with all of its acceptance criteria; \"nfrs\" each NFR you change or add; \"removed\" the ids you delete; \"outOfScope\" and \"assumptions\" the whole list, only when it changes. Everything you leave out stays exactly as it is."}